"""The house voice of Crimson Ledger: how every character talks, and the wording that does it.

Owner's decision (19 Nov 2026): Madam Shek Yu-lan's battle lines (strings.py battle.foe.agent.*) set the
standard for the whole cast. Every line a character says aloud is written in that style. This module
holds the rule (HOUSE_STYLE, for anyone writing new lines) and the rewritten lines (LINES), and lays
them over strings.EN at import. The wording each line replaces becomes its family-friendly wording
(strings.FAMILY), unless the line already had one, so the family switch still gives clean lines.

SPOKEN holds the voice's own versions (cast.SAY) for lines with amounts in them: the same joke,
without the numbers, so a line is recorded once and replayed for free.

New lines change the words, so the voice records them fresh the next time each one is heard; the old
clips stay in cache/voice/ and still play wherever the old wording is in use (family-friendly).
"""

# The rule. Follow it for every new spoken line, for every character.
HOUSE_STYLE = """\
Every character in Crimson Ledger talks like Madam Shek Yu-lan at sea: sexy, funny and quick.

1. Every spoken line carries a wink: a double meaning, a tease, a sly compliment, a velvet threat. The
   game fact comes first and stays plain (what happened, what it cost, what to do); the wink rides on it.
2. Innuendo, never explicit. Suggest, imply and leave the door ajar. No sex acts, no anatomy, nothing
   coarse enough to stop being funny. Let the listener finish the joke.
3. Each woman flirts in her own voice: Roxana dry and intimate, Powder bawdy and loud, Madam Wu
   velvet-warm, Li Yue cold and slow, Yu-lan silky and amused, Malcolm Haig dry Scots, Lau Siu-wah
   brisk and shrewd, Tang Wai-lun gruff and in love with timber, Inspector Vane foul-mouthed,
   Tommy Marsh shameless gossip, the Mayor regal, Mrs. Marlowe conspiratorial, each officer and warden
   as her card describes. The style is shared; the voices are not.
4. Reach for the trade's own words: holds, cargo, ledgers, figures, rates, interest, accounts, guns,
   powder, ramrods, broadsides, timber, keels, rigging, berths, cells, irons and keys all do double duty.
5. Short and quick. A one-line wink beats a speech. Lines that repeat often (receipts, rule messages)
   are the shortest of all, so they stay funny the twentieth time.
6. Call the player "Captain", "darling", "sweetheart" or "my dear"; never assume more about them.
7. Keep every {name} the line had, and no new ones. Spoken versions drop the amounts.
8. Everyone is an adult, always. The family-friendly wording keeps the wit and loses the innuendo.
"""

# A line for the voice itself, added to every character's own delivery note. Only the full TTS-2 model
# follows delivery notes (voice.steers); TTS-2 Flash ignores them and reads the words as written.
DELIVERY = "playful and knowing, savouring every double meaning with a smile in the voice"

LINES = {}
SPOKEN = {}

